ancient Sparta is famous above all for  its reputation as a military power its  most famous King Leonidas led the  fateful battle of thermopylae but one  thing that many people don't talk about  is the fact that he wasn't the only king  of Sparta it had two kings from two  different royal families who both held  equal power the question is why how did  Sparta come to have two kings and what  did they do  well the origins of the Spartan Dyer key  is debated and frankly not something  with a definitive answer  the Spartans believed that both their  royal dynasties the ickey ad in Europe  on today mum when King Aerys two demos  had twins nobody knew which one was  older and so the Spartans decided that  both should be king what many historians  believe is the most likely reason for  Sparta having two kings is that when the  city-state of Sparta was first founded  it was made up of the merger of smaller  settlements the rulers of the two  largest settlements came to an agreement  to have a dual monarchy which managed to  persist just like in ancient Crete of  course ask him why Sparta had two kings  isn't limited to asking where they came  from it also includes asking why they  kept this system for centuries and in  order to know why Sparta had two kings  you need to know how its political  system worked Sparta had what's called a  mixed Constitution it had an elected  chamber called the Apella from which the  gerousia or a Council of thirty elders  were selected which also included the  two kings whose positions were  hereditary yet despite there being kings  the most powerful people in Sparta were  the Air Force who were five people  elected from the entire population for a  one-year term and amongst the Air Force  there was often little disagreement  mostly because they all used their  position to get rich and destroy people  who didn't like them so what did spartan  kings actually do then that depends on  which Kings you're looking at and when  since over time Spartan Kings lost  certain rights and privileges and by the  time of the Persian War as they did to  things above all else they were  religious leaders and they led the army  whilst on campaign though Spartan Kings  had near absolute Authority which is why  Aristotle described them as being  hereditary generals at home though  spartan kings could do little except for  overseeing religious matters and voting  in the gerousia so why would Sparta keep  two kings then why not just have one or  even scrub the kings all together well  the reason the king stayed was largely  because of how politically weak they  were you see the Air Force were the only  ones with real power and if there was  only one king then that means that the  opposition to them could coalesce around  one man with two they only needed to  keep one on side and since the two  dynasties were often rivals for glory  and power that wasn't too hard  it also prevented one of them from  running the Army in thus becoming too  powerful this is why the f4 was made a  promise to uphold the dire key and would  prevent one dynasty from removing the  other had the Spartan Kings had more  power than frankly the system would have  collapsed earlier and when the dike he  came to an end it was replaced by a  monarchy in 210 BC sputter itself didn't  last much longer and in 192 BC it was  conquered and forever removed as an  independent state I hope you enjoyed  this episode and thank you for watching  but thanks to my patreon supporters whom  you can see on screen now and with an  extra special thanks to James  Bissonnette Ike Danny Maloney Colin  Castleman Marvin Castle Rob Waterhouse  Aaron the white Jordan long way Chris  wicker Gustav Swann Gareth Turner  Marcus arse nur Maggie pax Kowski David  Silverman spinning three plates  Spencer Lightfoot Lexi Schwinn Winston  k-word Kelly moneymaker  Anthony Beckett the original cage Robert  Wetzel Skye Chappelle par shoe 19 and Mo
 

